    "ls|LS16_S7_U_213": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 213 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 24.02.2016 \n \nSHORTAGE OF TRAINERS \n \nQUESTION \n \n213. SHRI K. PARASURAMAN: \n \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP be \npleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether due to shortage of adequate trainers, it is difficult to implement skill \ndevelopment programmes across the country; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n \n(a) the fields in which the number of trainers are less under this programme and \nwhether the Government has also planned to invite foreign collaboration in this regard; and \n \n(d)  if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) IN THE MINISTRY OF  \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n(SHRI RAJIV PRATAP RUDY) \n \n(a) to (d) Yes, Madam.  Government is aware that there is shortage of adequate \ntrainers to implement skill development programmes across the country; \n \nAt present, total seating capacity under different training schemes comes to about \n25 lakh(ITIs/ Apprenticeships/ Short -term courses) which requires about 1,25,000 trainers \nin the country. Presently about 25,000 trained instructors passed out from Central \nInstitutes under Craft Instructor Training Scheme (CITS) are available in the Country.  \n  \nFurther, t here are 30 Central I nstitutes and 16 Institut es for Training of Trainers  \n(Private & State Government) with an annual capacity of about 10,600 seats  and catering \nto the requirement of mostly manufacturing sector.  Further, the industry practitioners are \nalso being encouraged to join the training institutes as trainers. At present, my ministry is \ncollaborating with Chambers in Germany to train master trainers in the field of \nmechatronics and welding. \n \n \n******* \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S7_U_214": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 214\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 11TH MARCH, 2016\nCANCER INSTITUTES\n*214. SHRI BALABHADRA MAJHI:\nSHRI ARJUN MEGHWAL:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to\nstate:\n(a) whether the Government has received any proposal for establishing/ upgrading\nvarious National/Regional Cancer Care Centres to an Apex Centre of Excellence in\nthe country;\n(b) if so, the details thereof and the status of such proposals, State/UT-wise;\n(c) whether affiliation of these Centres are also under consideration of the\nGovernment; and\n(d) if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI JAGAT PRAKASH NADDA)\n(a) to (d):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 214* FOR 11TH MARCH, 2016\n(a)to (d) : References were received from the Government of Tamil Nadu regarding\nupgradation of the Cancer Institute (WIA), Adyar, as a Centre of Excellence - an\nAutonomous National Cancer Research Institute, from the Government of Kerala for\nupgradation of Regional Cancer Centre, Thiruvananthapuram to National Cancer\nInstitute  and from the Government of Rajasthan for setting up of National Cancer\nInstitute at Jaipur. Currently , Government of India is not implementing any scheme in\nthe field of Cancer for declaring a Cancer Institute as a Centre of Excellence. However,\nunder the 12 th Five Year Plan, National Cancer Institute at Jhajjar (Haryana) and 2 nd\ncampus of Chittranjan National Cancer Institute, Kolkata have been approved.\nThe Government of India is also implementing a scheme for enhancing the\ntertiary care facilities for Cancer in the country. Under the said scheme, Government of\nIndia assists to set up/establish State Cancer Institutes (SCI) and Tertiary Care Cancer\nCentre (TCCC) in different parts of the co untry. The maximum assistance inclusive of\nState share for SCI is upto Rs. 120 crores and for TCCC is upto Rs. 45 crores. Under\nthe said scheme, 47 proposals have been received out of which 16 proposals are for\nSCI and 31 for TCCCs. After evaluation and e xamination, 6 SCIs and 5 TCCCs have\nbeen approved where first installment of Central Government share has been released.\n…….",
    "ls|LS16_S7_U_216": "Government of India \nMinistry of Finance \nDepartment of Financial Services \nLOK SABHA\nStarred Question No. *216\n                     To be answered on11March, 2016/Phalguna21, 1937 (Saka)\nBank Accounts for Farmers\n*216. SHRI SUNIL KUMAR SINGH;\nSHRI SADASHIV LOKHANDE\nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the recent study/report of the National Bank for Agriculture and Rural \nDevelopment (NABARD) has indicated that nearly 52 per cent of farmers of the \ncountry do not have a bank account, if so, the details thereof and the reaction of the \nGovernment thereon; \n(b) whether the share of the agricultural loan to the North Eastern States is less than \nten per cent, if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government has any proposal to lower the interest rate on loans \nprovided to poor farmers belonging to Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes and \nOther Backward Classes, if so, the details thereof; and \n(d) the steps taken/being taken by the Government to provide easy loans to farmers \nin this regard?\nAnswer\nThe Finance Minister\n(Shri ArunJaitley)\n(a) to (d):-  A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n\nStatement referred to in reply to parts (a) to (d) of Lok Sabha Starred Question \nNo. *216 to be answered on 11thMarch, 2016 regarding Bank Accounts for \nFarmers\n(a): No Madam. However, the National Sample Survey Organisation (NSSO) \nconducted Situation Assessment Survey (SAS) of Agricultural Households during \nNSS 70th round for the reference period of the agricultural year July 2012- June \n2013, which estimated that approximately 52 per cent of agricultural households are \nindebted to either institutional or non-institutional sources. \n(b): Yes Madam. Though agricultural credit in India has registered consistent growth, \nregional variation in distribution of the credit remains, offtake of agriculture credit is \nhampered due to various reasons including topography,sparse settlements of \npopulation, lower level of commercialization, lack of irrigation facilities, infrastructural \nbottlenecks, more than one fifth of land being fallow, and weak cooperatives.\n(c)& (d): Various initiatives have been taken to reduce debt burden on farmers as \nwell as to provide easy and hassle-free loan to farmers, including short term crop \nloans at a low rate of 7% p.a. for which 2% subvention is given. An additional 3% \nsubvention for prompt repayment is also given by the Central Government so that \nthe effective rate of interest is reduced to 4%. In addition, targets are given to banks \nfor agriculture credit under Priority Sector Lending (PSL) Guidelines, ATM enabled \nKisan Credit Cards have been issued, Joint Liability Groups (JLGs) to bring small, \nmarginal, tenant farmers, oral lessees, etc. into the fold of institutional credit have \nbeen promoted, and an automatic mechanism put in place for initiating relief \nmeasures in areas affected by natural calamities.\n*******",
    "ls|LS16_S7_U_218": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF STATISTICS AND PROGRAMME IMPLEMENTATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.218 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON WEDNESDAY, THE 24th FEBRUARY, 2016 \n \nWEBSITE AND MOBILE APPLICATION \n \n218. SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR: \n  \n Will the Minister of STATISTICS AND PROGRAMME \nIMPLEMENTATION be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government plans to upgrade website for better \nvisual interaction and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b) if not, the reasons therefor; \n \n(c) whether the Government plans to build an mobile application to \nview freely available data and if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(d) if not, the reasons therefor? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE)  FOR MINISTRY OF  \nSTATISTICS AND PROGRAMME IMPLEMENTATION  AND MINISTER OF \nSTATE FOR MINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS  [GENERAL (Dr.) V. K. \nSINGH (Retd.)] \n \n(a) & (b)  Upgrading of website of the Ministry of Statistics &  \nProgramme Implementation is taken up from time to time, as per  the \nGuidelines for Indian Government Websites (GIGW), which ensure \ninter-alia better visual interaction. The upgraded website will be \naccessible by the visually challenged people also by using the freely \ndownloadable assistive “Screen Reader Access” tool. \n \n(c) At present, there is no provision for mobile application. \n(d) Mobile owners having internet access can access data freely. \n***** \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S7_U_219": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 219\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 11TH MARCH, 2016\nEXPANSION PLAN OF AIIMS\n†*219. SHRIMATI KRISHNA RAJ:\nSHRI SATAV RAJEEV:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to\nstate:\n(a) the average number of patients visiting AIIMS OPD on each day, department -\nwise;\n(b) whether the Government has approved expansion plan for AIIMS trauma centre\nand if so, the details thereof;\n(c) whether DDA has allotted land to AIIMS for expansion including trauma centre,\nif so, the details thereof;\n(d) the time by which the expansion plan i s likely to be completed and\noperationalised; and\n(e) the other steps taken by the Government for upgradation of the country's premier\nhealth institute?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI JAGAT PRAKASH NADDA)\n(a) to (e):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 219* FOR 11TH MARCH, 2016\n(a) 16.60 lakh patients have visited AIIMS OPD since July 1, 2015 till March 08,\n2016. Nearly 10,000 patients visit AIIMS, New Delhi , every day. Data on\naverage number of patients visiting AIIMS OPD, department -wise is not being\nmaintained.\n(b) to (d) DDA has allotted 14.95 acres of land next to the JPN Apex Trauma\nCentre to AIIMS, New Delhi. Expansion of various facilities including Trauma\nCentre at this piece of land is under consideration.\n(e) Some of the steps taken by the Government for upgradation of AIIMS, New\nDelhi include setting up of National Cancer Institute; a new  Mother & Child\nBlock; a new OPD Block; and a new Hostel Block.\n***",
    "ls|LS16_S7_U_22": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 22 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FEBRUARY 24, 2016 \n \nAPPROVAL TO STATE LEVEL PLANS \n \nNo.22               SHRIMATI K.MARAGATHAM:  \n \nWill the Minister of URBAN DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has approved State level plans unlike \nthe past practice of appraisi ng and approving individual \nprojects;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Governments has requ ested the States to install \nwater meters at the homes of consumers; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT \n (SHRI BABUL SUPRIYO) \n \n(a) & (b): Yes, Madam. Under th e Atal Mission for Rejuvenation \nand Urban     Transformation (A MRUT), State Annual Action Plans \n(SAAPs) of 20 States have been approved with total project costs \nof Rs.19996.08 crore involving Central assistance of Rs. 9091.71 \nCrore. The Mission does not require the State/UT Govts to submit \nto Central Government indivi dual projects for appraisal and \napproval. \nCont…. \n\n-2- \n(c) & (d): The AMRUT mandates ci ties to adopt a policy on user \nchares for water use with adequate safeguards to take care \nof the interest of the vulner able as one of the reforms. The \nMission incentivizes accomplishment of reforms by setting \naside 10% of annual budgetary allocation from second year \nonwards to be given as incentive. \n**** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S7_U_220": "O.I.H \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN POVERTY ALLEVIATION  \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 220 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FEBRUARY 24, 2016 \n \nHOUSES ON RENT \n \n \nNo. 220 \n  \n \nSHRI SHARAD TRIPATHI \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN POVERTY ALLEVIATION be \npleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware that the slum-dwellers who have \nbeen allotted houses in the cities have rented out these \naccommodation and are again living in a slums;  and \n \n(b) if so, the action taken by the Government in this regard? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOUSING & URBAN \nPOVERTY ALLEVIATION \n(SHRI BABUL SUPRIYO) \n \n (a) & (b): Housing, Slum and colonization are State subjects. The \nCentral Government through its schemes assist State/UT/City \nGovernments in facilitating housing for the urban poor including slum \ndwellers. No such matter re garding renting out of these \naccommodation by slum dwellers has been brought to the notice of \nthe Ministry by the State/UT Governments. \n \n**** ",
    "ls|LS16_S7_U_221": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF STATISTICS AND PROGRAMME \nIMPLEMENTATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 221 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON WEDNESDAY, THE 24TH FEBRUARY, \n2016 \n \nMONITORING OF INFRASTRUCTURE PROJECTS \n \n221.  SHRI B.V. NAIK: \n \nWill the Minister of STATISTICS AND PROGRAMME \nIMPLEMENTATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is monitoring projects especially \nthose   belonging to infrastructure sector;   \n(b) if so, how many projects are behind schedule with cost \noverruns; and  \n(c) whether any decisions have been taken for their early \ncompletion and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR MINISTRY OF \nSTATISTICS AND PROGRAMME IMPLEMENTATION AND MINISTER \nOF STATE FOR MINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS [GENERAL (Dr.) \nV.K. SINGH (Retd.)] \n \n(a) : Government through the Ministry of Statistics and Programme \nImplementation monitors on -going Central Sector Infrastructure \nProjects costing Rs. 150 crore and above on time and cost overruns \non the basis of information provided by the project implementation \nagencies.  \n \n(b) :   As on 01.01.2016, a total of 1058 projects were on the monitor \nof this Ministry. Of these, 77 projects were showing both time and \ncost overruns with respect to their original project implementation  \nschedules. Details are at Annexure. \n \n\n(c) :   The major steps undertaken to ensure completion of Central \nSector       Infrastructure Projects without time and cost overruns \ninclude rigorous project appraisal; On -line Computerized Monitoring \nSystem (OCMS) f or better monitoring; setting up of Standing \nCommittees in the Ministries for fixation of responsibility for time \nand cost overruns; regular review of infrastructure projects by the \nconcerned administrative Ministries; and setting up of Central \nSector Proj ects Coordination Committees (CSPCCs) in the States \nunder the Chief Secretaries for removal of bottlenecks and for \nfacilitating the speedy implementation of major projects.   \n \n\nAnnexure \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (b) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO 221  DUE FOR ANSWER ON  24.02.2016  \nDETAILS OF CENTRAL SECTOR PROJECTS SHOWING BOTH TIME AND COST OVERRUNS (as on 01.01.2016)  \nSN PROJECT IMPLEMENTING \nAGENCY \nDATE OF \nAPPROVAL \n(month/year) \nORIGINAL DATE \nOF \nCOMMISSIONING \n(month/year) \nANTICIPATED \nDATE OF \nCOMMISSIONING \n(month/year) \nCOST \nORIGINAL \n(Rs. In \ncrore) \nCOST \nANTICIPATED \n(Rs. In crore) \n REPORTED \nCUMULATIVE \nEXPENDITURE  \n(Rs. In crore) \nCOST \nOVERRUN \n(%) \nTIME \nOVERRUN \n(MONTHS) \n  ATOMIC ENERGY \n1 PROTOTYPE FAST \nBREEDER REACTOR \n(BHAVINI, 500 MWE)                                                    \nBHAVNI 09/2003 09/2010 09/2016 3492.00 5677.00 5129.59 62.57 72 \n2 KUDANKULAM APP (NPCIL)                                                                               NPCIL 12/2001 12/2008 06/2016 13171.00 22462.00 21025.00 70.54 90 \n  COAL \n3 NIGAHI OC EXPANSION \nPROJECT 10 MTY TO 15 \nMTY, SINGRAULI                                              \nNCL 10/2007 03/2012 03/2017 259.40 486.57 75.15 87.58 60 \n4 RFR OF MANUGURU OC \nPROJECT                                                                           \nSCCL 11/2012 03/2016 03/2018 430.45 486.27 93.51 12.97 24 \n5 RAMAGUNDAM  OC-II \nEXTENSION                                                                          \nSCCL 12/2009 03/2012 03/2023 418.97 489.62 411.47 16.86 132 \n6 ADRIYALA SHAFT PROJECT                                                                               SCCL 12/2009 03/2013 03/2016 212.34 1228.39 866.72 478.50 36 \n  HEAVY INDUSTRY \n7 NATIONAL AUTOMOTIVE \nTESTING AND  RD \nINFRASTRUCTURE \nPROJECT (NATRIP)                                  \nHIPE 04/2011 09/2011 12/2017 1718.00 3827.30 0.00 122.78 75 \n  PETROCHEMICALS \n8 ASSAM GAS CRACKER \nPROJECT                                            